# The co-occurrence refusal pools the fields read off one value and asks
# whether any declared record holds them all. That is only sound for reads
# that run whenever the body runs, and this fixture is the other three cases —
# each of them a correct program the rule would refuse if it pooled too much.
#
# `describe` reads a span's field in one arm of an `if` and a node's in the
# other. Only one arm is taken, so the value is one record either way; pooling
# both would find no record holding `from` and `kind` and refuse a program that
# works.
#
# `widths` shadows: the outer `m` is a node and the lambda's `m` is a span.
# The lambda may never be called at all, and its parameter is a different value
# from the one outside, so its reads belong to neither pool.
#
# `both` rebinds `p` from a span to a node. Reads before and after the binding
# are of different values, and pooling across it would gather all four fields.
#
# Break any of the three and this fixture stops printing; that is what it is
# here to catch.
import "std/io"
import "std/list"

type span
  from
  to

type node
  kind
  wanted

fn describe x flag
  if flag "{x.from}" "{x.kind}"

fn widths m items
  "{m.kind}:{list/to_list (list/map items (m -> m.from + m.to))}"

both =
  p = span 1 5
  first = "{p.from} {p.to}"
  p = node 2 3
  "{first} {p.kind} {p.wanted}"

pub play =
  io/write "{describe (span 1 5) true}\n"
  io/write "{describe (node 2 3) false}\n"
  io/write "{widths (node 7 8) [(span 1 5) (span 2 6)]}\n"
  io/write "{both}\n"
